#!/bin/sh
# omacosy-finder-window — open a NEW Finder window on the CURRENT workspace.
#
# What Super+Shift+F used to run was `open ~`, which has two problems.
#
# `open <dir>` RAISES an existing window for that directory rather than
# making a new one, and the binding always asks for the home folder, so it
# can only ever produce ONE Finder window. Measured from no windows open:
#
#   open ~             -> 1 window
#   open ~ again       -> 1 window
#   open ~/Downloads   -> 2 windows, tiled side by side
#
# The limit was never Finder or AeroSpace. It was asking for the same
# folder twice.
#
# Raising that window also ACTIVATES Finder, and AeroSpace follows the
# frontmost app to whichever workspace its window is on. So the key drags
# the desktop to wherever a Finder window already lives, and anything
# opened after that lands there instead of where you were.
#
# `open -n` is not the answer. For Finder it fails with "Launch failed" and
# takes the existing window down with it. Measured twice.
#
# ORDER MATTERS in what follows.
#
# `activate` has to come AFTER the window exists. Without it at all, the
# window appears but Finder is never brought forward, no focus change
# fires, and `omacosy-helper split-hint` never runs — so AeroSpace appends
# the window to the existing row instead of splitting the focused slot.
# Measured: split-hint ran 0 times against 1 for a normal `open -n -a`.
#
# Activating after the window exists is what keeps the workspace still:
# Finder comes forward with the NEW window frontmost, and that window is
# already here, so AeroSpace has nowhere to follow it to.
#
# KNOWN LIMIT, not fixable here: a Finder window will not go below 474
# points wide. Asked for 300 through AppleScript, it returns 474. In a
# narrower slot its windows overlap and run off the display. The same slots
# with a terminal gave an exact fit. Three Finder windows suit a 1440-point
# display (3 x 474 = 1422); two beside another app do not.
#
# Needs an Automation grant ("... wants to control Finder"), once, on first
# use. That is the trade for the two bugs above.
#
# >/dev/null: osascript echoes the folder it set.
exec osascript -e 'tell application "Finder"
  set w to make new Finder window
  set target of w to path to home folder
  activate
end tell' >/dev/null
